cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Swiss route choice: modelling taste heterogeneity and its small-sample limits"
message: "If you use this code or its findings, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - alias: pogbonna-IAL
    email: pogbonna@gmail.com
repository-code: "https://github.com/pogbonna-IAL/swiss-route-choice"
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  Four specifications of taste heterogeneity in a stated-preference route
  choice panel -- a baseline multinomial logit, covariate-interacted
  coefficients, a correlated mixed logit with negative lognormal parameters,
  and latent class models with constant and covariate-driven allocation --
  fitted to identical data so their likelihoods are comparable. A resampling
  experiment then refits every model at sample sizes from 250 respondents down
  to 5, twenty draws each, to establish where latent class estimation stops
  being reproducible. Across 1,044 fits, every one converged and fewer than
  half were usable; class identity is the first thing to fail, at 250
  respondents, where every conventional diagnostic still reads green.
keywords:
  - discrete choice
  - mixed logit
  - latent class
  - taste heterogeneity
  - reproducibility
  - small sample
  - apollo
references:
  - type: software
    title: "Apollo: a flexible, powerful and customisable freeware package for choice model estimation and application"
    authors:
      - family-names: Hess
        given-names: Stephane
      - family-names: Palma
        given-names: David
    year: 2019
    doi: 10.1016/j.jocm.2019.100170
    notes: >-
      Supplies both the estimation engine and the
      apollo_swissRouteChoiceData dataset used throughout.
